{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>You shall not see your brother's ox or his sheep driven away</b> — this is a commandment already stated explicitly in the Torah, where it says (Exodus 23:4) <b>If you meet your enemy's ox or his donkey going astray, you shall surely bring it back to him</b>. Here He added the word <b>נִדָּחִים</b> (driven away), because <b>תּוֹעֶה</b> (going astray) means it wandered from its path and one can turn it back without great effort, whereas now He mentioned <b>נִדָּחִים</b>, meaning they fled from him and went far away. And He mentioned the sheep, which is like a lost item, and therefore He explained here, <b>and if your brother is not near you, or if you do not know him, then you shall gather it into your house</b>. And He said, <b>and so shall you do for his donkey</b> — which is an unclean animal; <b>and so shall you do for his garment</b> — even though the garment is not as precious to its owner as living animals are to theirs, and the loss involved in it is not as consequential as with them, since it is possible for animals to die; <b>and so shall you do for every lost thing of your brother's</b> — for every utensil of his house, even though they are not as precious as his garment with which he covers himself. And according to the midrash of our Sages (Sifrei; Baba Metzia 30a), many additional matters are derived here, such as: <b>you shall hide yourself</b> — sometimes you may hide yourself; <b>you shall bring back</b> — even a hundred times (Baba Metzia 31a); <b>you shall bring them back</b> — even to his garden or his ruin (ibid.); and returning it by means of identifying marks; and other matters are expounded in the passage:"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:14:57.022366481Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}